you know, one, property owners are encouraged to implement additional stormwater mitigation. If they're out there building a house,

00:29:40.455 --> 00:29:42.135
that's the ideal time to,

00:29:42.695 --> 00:29:45.255
you know, put in dry wells and that sort of thing,

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:51.030
why they're doing it, and we would encourage them to do those kind of extra

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:55.830
actions. And then the last paragraph here talks about actions taken by property owners

00:29:57.910 --> 00:30:04.235
may be considered in fulfilling future measures required by further future development so that

00:30:05.035 --> 00:30:14.555
that allows the good citizens to get a little bit of credit for any good work they did. So that covers all the points that I wanted to about stormwater runoff.

00:30:20.790 --> 00:30:22.470
We're ready to move into

00:30:22.550 --> 00:30:23.670
septic

00:30:24.310 --> 00:30:25.110
changes

00:30:25.110 --> 00:30:25.830
here.

00:30:29.110 --> 00:30:35.510
And I got a little bit of feedback from our

00:30:33.535 --> 00:30:35.135
town's sanitarian

00:30:35.135 --> 00:30:36.815
fad king today.

00:30:39.135 --> 00:30:39.775
So

00:30:40.255 --> 00:30:44.095
when we get there, I'll talk about what he suggests. I think it's

00:30:44.575 --> 00:30:46.335
pretty easy. But

00:30:47.760 --> 00:30:49.280
the intent and purpose

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:51.200
talks into our

00:30:51.440 --> 00:30:52.640
small lots,

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:54.800
old septic systems.

00:30:55.280 --> 00:30:59.600
It says the majority of septic systems were installed prior to 1980. And

00:31:00.055 --> 00:31:07.335
I was always curious what the magic date of 1980 was. Maybe septic tanks wear out after forty years, I don't know. But

00:31:07.735 --> 00:31:09.255
it turns out that

00:31:11.575 --> 00:31:13.015
around 1980,

00:31:13.015 --> 00:31:14.775
maybe 1982,

00:31:14.935 --> 00:31:16.135
is when the state's

00:31:16.640 --> 00:31:20.400
septic tank public health codes were put into effect.

00:31:22.160 --> 00:31:26.480
And so when I was talking with Thad King about

00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:28.960
the goals for our regulations

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:29.280
here,

00:31:30.235 --> 00:31:35.675
He was just wanting to make sure that we weren't going to get everybody a new septic system overnight.

00:31:36.955 --> 00:31:41.595
He said that either in his health district or the entire state, I can't remember,

00:31:43.950 --> 00:31:44.990
are 20,000

00:31:44.990 --> 00:31:46.590
septic tanks right now,

00:31:47.550 --> 00:31:48.510
and probably

00:31:48.670 --> 00:31:50.030
only 10,000

00:31:50.030 --> 00:31:52.510
of those do they have any documentation

00:31:52.510 --> 00:31:53.150
of

00:31:53.390 --> 00:31:58.510
to know whether or not they're in compliance with the rules that have come into effect. So they've been working

00:31:58.595 --> 00:32:00.675
on that since 1980,

00:32:00.995 --> 00:32:02.915
and they're about halfway there now.

00:32:04.195 --> 00:32:05.715
I thought it was interesting,

00:32:05.795 --> 00:32:07.155
you know, we can

00:32:07.635 --> 00:32:08.995
make requirements

00:32:08.995 --> 00:32:12.515
that are more significant than the public health code

00:32:12.675 --> 00:32:13.395
if we want.

00:32:14.120 --> 00:32:16.760
He mentioned, you know, the state of Massachusetts,

00:32:16.760 --> 00:32:19.720
if somebody sells a piece of property up there

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:23.480
and the septic system doesn't meet the existing code,

00:32:24.280 --> 00:32:26.440
that has to be remediated

00:32:28.775 --> 00:32:33.495
for the sale of the house. And Connecticut doesn't require that, but

00:32:35.815 --> 00:32:36.455
there you go.

00:32:41.960 --> 00:32:44.760
Okay. So now we get in and

00:32:52.280 --> 00:32:52.840
was there?

